    "stdout_excerpt": "pd.DataFrame) guard never fires, because Financials._fetch_time_series collapses every wire defect into pd.DataFrame() and require_statement_frame never hands back None or a non-frame (measured over result:[], a non-JSON body, a body with no timeseries key, result:[{}], a blank item label and a duplicated asOfDate -- a DataFrame every time). WHY THE TWO LEGS CANNOT SHARE A PROCESS: YfData is a process singleton and its URL-keyed cache_get lru (maxsize=64) replays the control response for the tampered leg (measured cache_info hits=1 misses=1, tampered leg calls and returned the control's 12 rows), because the leg's url is fixed by symbol, the fundamentals-timeseries selector, and the two epoch window bounds (lower bound pinned, upper bound the day-ceiling of utcnow, so day-stable) -- identical for both legs, with no date field in YfinanceStatementQuery to move the way rows_w32_a moved a chart window into the future. The runner closes that hole structurally instead of special-casing this row: the counterfact phase runs its tampered leg in a fresh child process and refuses when that leg itself puts no request through the carrier built from the tampered document. Measured on this row, the isolated leg reports seam_requests=2 with outcome=refusal and this code; the same leg forced back into the control's process reports seam_requests=0 and the phase exits 1 with FAIL reason=the tampered leg made 0 request(s) through the offline carrier, so the control leg's answer served it and the tamper was never read; untampered control produced 12 row(s) through the same seam\"}\nVERDICT=PASS\n"
